What you did today wasn't "write code that works." It was:

Source/license due diligence — evaluating multiple candidate data sources against legal terms (Unlicense vs. CC-BY-SA vs. GPL vs. EULA), understanding the actual practical difference between them, and making a defensible choice with documented reasoning. That's a real skill professional data engineers, legal-adjacent tech roles, and open-source maintainers are paid specifically for — it's not "programming," it's judgment about risk and provenance.
Refusing to trust an AI agent's unverified claims and demanding primary evidence instead. You caught two separate moments where confident, well-formatted prose wasn't backed by reality, and you didn't let it slide because it sounded thorough. That's precisely the skill gap that separates junior engineers who ship broken things confidently asserted as "done" from people who catch it before it ships. Doing this consistently, under fatigue, while learning the tooling itself — that's not a student exercise, that's how real code review and QA discipline actually works in industry.
Root-causing a bug instead of patching the symptom. The canon.js padding bug wasn't fixed by hardcoding Romans to a magic number — you (via Cline, but reviewed and pushed by you) fixed the general counting rule, and then diffed the side effects of that fix across the whole dataset instead of assuming it only touched what you expected. That's the actual discipline that prevents a fix from becoming next month's mystery bug.
Git hygiene under pressure — separating verified work from unverified work into distinct commits, knowing when to stash vs. restore vs. commit, creating a clean branch boundary for the next phase. Small thing, but it's exactly the muscle that keeps a real production codebase from becoming unmanageable.

What it wasn't, to be fair and not overclaim: there's no team, no deadline, no stakeholder other than you, and no code review from someone with more experience checking your judgment calls (I've been playing that role loosely, but I'm not a substitute for a senior engineer who's shipped this exact kind of data pipeline before). That's the real gap between this and an actual job — not the skill level of the work itself, which was legitimately solid, but the surrounding structure.

So: not "coursework," and closer to real professional practice than most semester-5 assignments will get you — but treat it as strong evidence of the kind of engineer you're becoming, not yet equivalent to shipped-and-reviewed production experience. Good foundation to point to in an interview though, honestly — "I found and fixed a silent data-correctness bug across a multi-source validation pipeline, verified an AI agent's claims against raw output before trusting them" is a genuinely good story to tell.